A1. The second of a famous trinity found in cabarets. ~ WOMEN
A6. Mother used to make these for the table. ~ DOILY
A11. The advantage a greenhouse has over a slaughterhouse. ~ FRAGRANCE
A14. Pet name for Elizabeth. ~ BETSY
A16. What you wish your saxophone playing neighbor would do. ~ CEASE
A18. Turn me backward. ~ EM
A19. A famous river in France. ~ SEINE
A22. A preposition. ~ TO
A23. Potato. ~ SPUD
A25. The trouble with all egotists. ~ EGO
A26. The spread of an arch between abutments. ~ SPAN
A27. And (French). ~ ET
A28. Middle Western State (abr.). ~ IA
A30. A pronoun and the most frequently used word when two flappers are conversing. ~ HE
A31. The initials of a province in Canada. ~ NS
A32. This kind of prospective father-in-law talks with his feet. ~ IRATE
A33. Paregoric at 2 A.M. ~ COLIC
A34. The definite article that precedes nouns beginning with a vowel. ~ AN
A35. The hardest word in the English language to say. ~ NO
A37. A Greek letter that in mathematics means 3.1416. ~ PI
A38. An expression used when you find the correct word in a crossword puzzle. ~ HA
A40. A flat topped hill or plateau. ~ MESA
A41. A spot on a playing card. ~ PIP
A43. An essential attribute of a dealer in gold bricks. ~ GLIB
A44. A conjunction commonly used for comparison. ~ AS
A45. What every "Hubby" is expected to do with money. ~ AMASS
A47. Something girls get when crossing a street on a windy day. ~ OO
A48. A small island off the coast of Florida. ~ ISLET
A51. A word much used in business condition reports meaning direction of progress. ~ TREND
A54. What "Seventeen" feels for his snub-nosed, gum-chewing flapper. ~ ADORATION
A57. A poisonous snake. ~ ADDER
A58. Commonly thought of as "She who blushes." ~ BRIDE

D2. When a flapper likes to be kissed (poetical). ~ OFT
D3. Something most girls want before their name. ~ MRS
D4. This one is almost easy. ~ EAYS
D5. Don't try to cash this kind of cheque (abr.). ~ NG
D6. The first expression of the infant prodigy. ~ DA
D7. The first word of every fairy tale. ~ ONCE
D8. Hard water. ~ ICE
D9. What the lowing herd winds slowly o'er. ~ LEA
D10. The kind of a girl that men forget. ~ OBESE
D12. Something Kaiser Bill ain't gonna do no more. ~ REIGN
D13. The length of time between pay days when you're broke. ~ AEONS
D15. What you find in the streets of Edinburg on tag day! ~ EMPTINESS
D17. A support or post. ~ STANCHION
D20. Exclamation of a lady when she sees a mouse. ~ EE
D21. Gentle reply of a coy damsel when asked for a kiss. ~ NO
D24. The goddess of the chase. ~ DIANA
D26. The name of a motion picture concern. ~ SELIG
D29. A division of a town or ward among the Bantoc Igorrottes. ~ ATO
D30. Chinaman's delight. ~ HOP
D34. The manner in which a disgruntled better half wields a rolling-pin. ~ AMAIN
D36. Something a duchess wears on her hair (not stacomb). ~ TIARA
D39. A dwelling place. ~ ABODE
D41. Title of a small town postal card reader (abr.). ~ PM
D42. We call her this because her name is Adeline More (abr.). ~ PS
D45. Something a baby puts in its mouth (two words). ~ ATOE
D46. What you have to do to make good fudge. ~ STIR
D49. A small boy. ~ LAD
D50. Contraction of Edward with too many "d's." ~ EDD
D52. King (French). ~ ROI
D53. The tip of a pup's tail. ~ END
D55. Railroad (abr.). ~ RR
D56. Something to go to Denver for (abr.). ~ TB


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
D29: A division of a town or ward among the Bantoc Igorrottes. → Bontoc Igorrotes
D4: This one is almost easy. → EAY (answer as printed; 'easy' with a letter missing, the joke of the clue)

